        Two bugs in the assembly, both found by running it.

        Both were found on arm64, which is the architecture this machine can
        execute. Both are present in the x86_64 and riscv64 blocks as the same
        instruction sequence, and neither has been executed there: there is no
        user mode qemu here. What follows says which claims are measured and
        which are read off the page.

        ----------------------------------------------------------------
        1. string_find walked past matches.               ALL THREE ARCHES

        moonwater_find is two loops: an outer one hunting the input's first
        byte, an inner one walking the two strings while they agree. When the
        inner one failed it jumped back into the outer one leaving the scan
        pointer where the mismatch happened, with a comment saying "carry on
        from here, as the C did".

        A candidate that fails has to restart one byte past where it began.
        Carrying on from the mismatch steps over every start inside the part
        that did match, so "aab" is not found in "aaab": the candidate at zero
        eats two bytes before failing, and the scan resumes past the one at
        one that would have matched.

        This needs no unusual input. Over strings drawn from a four letter
        alphabet it missed 1066 of 200000 searches, about one in two hundred,
        and repetitive text makes it worse. string_find is what grep and sed
        use for fixed string search.

        Fixed on all three by restarting at the candidate's own start plus
        one. Measured after: 201778 searches, no misses.  [arm64 executed]

        ----------------------------------------------------------------
        2. The byte hunts lost a match to a borrow.               ARM64

        strchr, strchrnul and strnchr all align the pointer down so the first
        eight byte load cannot cross a page, then throw away the bytes before
        the string by ANDing the finished flag word with a mask.

        The SWAR zero test is (v - 0x0101..) & ~v & 0x8080... A zero byte in
        it borrows into the byte above, which can come out flagged holding no
        zero at all. The identity is exact for "is there a zero" and for
        "where is the lowest one", and not for anything above the lowest.
        Masking the flags afterwards removes the true flag in the prefix and
        keeps the false one it caused in the first byte of the string.

        So a byte just before the string equal to the one being hunted, and a
        string beginning with the next value up, and the routine answers about
        a byte that is not there:

            prefix 0x02, string begins 0x03, hunting 0x02
                strchr      returns null    -- the match further on is missed
                strchrnul   returns s       -- the string reads as empty
                strnchr     returns null

        The same shape with 0x00 before the string and 0x01 at its start
        drives the terminator hunt instead, so strchrnul reports every string
        empty and callers walking with it stop on the first line.

        It cannot happen on an eight byte aligned string, which is why random
        data does not find it: 400000 random trials found none. Over the 7
        misalignments x 255 byte values that can reach it, strchrnul was wrong
        2667 times of 3570, strnchr 2667 of 3570, strchr 889 of 1785.

        The comment above arm64's strchr explains why the mask was chosen --
        forcing the prefix to 0xff the way strlen does would false-match a
        hunt for 0xff -- so this was a known trade made in the wrong
        direction. It is only true of the word: after the exclusive-or, 0xff
        means "differs from the byte in every bit", which holds whatever the
        byte is, 0xff included. So each hunt now forces its own input rather
        than sharing a mask over the flags, before the loop rather than in it,
        and the loop came out two instructions shorter than it was.

        Measured after: 910710 checks over both shapes and a random sweep of
        every alignment and length, no failures.        [arm64 executed]

        ----------------------------------------------------------------
        Still to check, and not claimed either way

        x86_64's memchr masks its flags the same way (and %r8, %rax) and is
        the same shape as the three above. x86_64's strlen and strnlen and
        riscv64's strlen force the input instead and are safe by inspection.
        riscv64's strchr and strchrnul have not been read closely.

        None of that can be settled here. It wants verify.c under the repo's
        own qemu, or MOONWATER_BUILD_HOST.
